-35459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124885 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $D$, given by\n$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124885 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a6c1395-93b3-574c-b784-2d33009b00c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124905 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124905 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124905 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124905 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c93d2ae4-7e7b-5500-9b27-fd939892ffa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124905 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125332 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125332 #edb/ref 1061 17200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125332 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9d24104-f86a-5d95-8475-56e2dbec3b1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125332 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125333 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125333 #edb/ref 1061 17201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125333 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "791285ec-5594-542a-8408-63f1e171f030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125333 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125334 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125334 #edb/ref 1061 17202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125334 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a8ae7cc9-0379-5556-914f-78c73a269672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125334 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125335 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125335 #edb/ref 1061 17203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125335 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c86295bd-d79e-5f83-97d7-9a828e1b01e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125335 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125336 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125336 #edb/ref 1061 17204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125336 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bd0dda5-f697-5c86-9873-bdf79b5a2e7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125336 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125337 #edb/ref 1061 17205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125337 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "097f2290-0ddc-5eda-9104-3480eee4084e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125337 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125757 #edb/ref 1064 "The **pivots** of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nFor example, given the matrix \n![](images/ad/addf5cd1e1219a1e1ce28f3d27d1b000b3a99640ff89a62e55b66f3a5cdb3521.svg)\nwhich is in row echelon form, let's find the pivots of $A.$\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the first row is the entry $a_{11}=1.$ So this is a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry of the second row is $a_{22}=1.$ So this is also a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the third row is $a_{34}=1.$ Therefore this is a pivot.\n\nThe pivots of $A$ are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/96/96dcf8cf9f0d387e0539ea04dd7ae4a498534361ad6bd5ed3bb119ae21f0416a.svg)\n\nThe **pivot columns** of a matrix in row echelon form are simply the columns in which the pivots appear.\n\nFor our matrix $A$, we can see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns.\n\nThe non-pivot columns are the columns that do not contain a pivot. So for the matrix $A,$ the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125757 #edb/ref 1065 3157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125757 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "feec6418-f447-5ee0-8c62-7d6b690f9228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125757 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125758 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the pivots and pivot columns of a general matrix, we first need to convert the matrix to row echelon form (REF).\n\n- The pivots of any matrix are the pivots of its row equivalent REF matrix.\n\n- The pivot columns of a matrix are the pivot columns of its row equivalent REF matrix.\n\nTo reduce a general $n\\times m$ matrix to row echelon form, we follow a similar procedure to the $2\\times 2$ and $3\\times 3$ cases, as we'll see in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125758 #edb/ref 1065 3158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125758 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d45a4eba-96ce-5bef-92b7-b8f0d4cd8b72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125758 #edb/ref 1067 "Pivot Columns of a General Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263117 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263117 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263117 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c423f32-6ae0-5d69-9e82-133ca26c00b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263345 #edb/ref 1080 "$1$st, $2$nd, and $5$th only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263345 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263345 #edb/ref